1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,600
Hi, I'm Tom from the Burp Scanner development team. Today I'm going to talk a little bit about

2
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:10,680
automatic session handling in Burp Scanner. Now this is something I've talked about previously

3
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:14,040
regarding the crawl, but today I'm going to focus a little bit more on how the audit

4
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:19,080
automatically maintains session. So let's dive straight in with an example app that shows why

5
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:25,560
this is so important. So I've just created a little application here that has two pages.

6
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:31,260
First we're given a page with a greet me link and if we click on that we're given a page with some

7
00:00:31,260 --> 00:00:38,220
pretty obvious reflection. However through some pretty basic cookie usage this page will only

8
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:45,600
allow five visits. So just keep refreshing it before eventually it says no. It gives me a 400 and says

9
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:55,080
please stop. So that means that if we go and actively scan this page, so if we do an active scan,

10
00:00:55,080 --> 00:01:01,740
we're not going to find the reflection because when we start sending payloads to the page,

11
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:09,300
we've already used up our session for that page and we start getting 400s. Now if you're getting

12
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:14,400
400s, if you're not hitting the application proper, your payloads aren't reaching the attack surface and

13
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:19,140
you're unlikely to find any bugs and this is why session handling is so important. Now we could set

14
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:24,900
up a manual session handling rule here or what we could do is we could crawl and audit our application.

15
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:30,660
So if I open this up and I don't want HTTPS, I just want HTTP and I go to localhost,

16
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:41,280
8000 and I scan that. So what's going to happen here is Burp Crawler is going to explore the

17
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:48,060
application and it's going to find our vulnerable page and not only is it going to find it, it's

18
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:52,740
also going to figure out what steps it has to take to get back there again should it no longer be in

19
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:59,520
session. This information will be handed off to the audit and the audit will then try to find the

20
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:04,260
shortest viable path that still gets us there. So in this case, nine times out of ten or I guess

21
00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:09,900
five times out of six, actually just hitting the same page over and again will work and it will

22
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:13,740
figure out, the audit will figure out that for the most part it could just keep sending the base

23
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:20,340
request and sending payloads in there. However, as those payload containing requests start to get

24
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:25,140
versions of the page that doesn't recognize, that it doesn't see as matching how it originally saw the

25
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:30,880
response when it was crawling, what it will do is it will stop and say, hmm, am I out of session?

26
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:36,360
The way it works this out is we go back to sending an unmodified base request. We want to work out

27
00:02:36,360 --> 00:02:42,220
whether it's the payload that's causing an unexpected response or whether our session has died. So we will

28
00:02:42,220 --> 00:02:47,580
send an unmodified base request. We will check to see if we're still in session. If we are, we'll go,

29
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:50,960
okay, well it's maybe just that our payloads are causing the application to hand us back different

30
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:56,360
responses, which is totally expected. If we're instead out of session, what we'll do is we'll go

31
00:02:56,360 --> 00:03:01,220
back to the full path to get back to that location and we will re-walk it so that we go and get a

32
00:03:01,220 --> 00:03:05,540
brand new session. After that we can go back to doing our simple path and keep sending our base

33
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:11,060
requests, keep putting payloads in it until it happens again. And in this way Burp Scanner will

34
00:03:11,060 --> 00:03:16,280
continually make sure that all of the payloads it sends are in session. If it ever works out that it

35
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:20,940
has lost session, it will roll back to the last time it saw a successful session, last time

36
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:25,800
it saw a payload being sent and getting a correct response and it will rerun any scan checks that

37
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:30,600
have been seeing unsuccessful responses since then, just to make sure that we're getting maximum

38
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:35,700
coverage. So as we can see here from our crawler and audit, we found our reflection and we found

39
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:40,800
the cross-site scripting that goes along with it. Now this was a topic that was requested a lot by the

40
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:46,440
community because it's not something that we talk about a great deal. If you have any more

41
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:50,220
suggestions for things you'd like me to cover, just let us know.

42
